{
 "schema": "nmsk.muscle_actions.v1",
 "round": "Q283",
 "doc": "Derivation table: each row maps ONE quoted action of a muscle (verbatim from data/muscles/<region>/<muscle>_r.json 'actions', or from a cited source in 'cites') to pose-rig degrees of freedom (joint, dof, sign: +1 = the dof's positive direction as labelled in data/anatomy/pose_rig_*.json, e.g. hip flex + = flexion, hip abd + = abduction, hip rot + = internal rotation, ankle dp + = dorsiflexion, subtalar inv + = inversion, forearm sup + = supination, wrist dev + = radial deviation). Joint names without side are resolved to <joint>_<side> of the picked muscle; spine regions (lumbar/thoracic/cervical) carry a 4th element 1 when the sign is for the RIGHT muscle and is mirrored for the left (lateral bend and rotation). 'c' = the functional compartment (suffix of its id) the row is limited to; rows without 'c' apply to the whole muscle and to every compartment. 'back' = animate from the opposite direction back to the model pose. Checked by scripts/anatomy/q283/build_learn.py (every quote must occur verbatim in its source).",
 "cites": {
  "kumazaki2022": {
   "citation": "Kumazaki T, Takahashi T, Nakano T, Sakai T. Action and Contribution of the Iliopsoas and Rectus Femoris as Hip Flexor Agonists Examined with Anatomical Analysis. Juntendo Iji Zasshi 2022;68(4):352-362",
   "doi": "10.14789/jmj.JMJ22-0009-OA",
   "pmid": "39021428",
   "pmcid": "PMC11250012",
   "quote": "The iliopsoas (psoas major and iliacus), rectus femoris, sartorius, and adductor longus are known as the hip flexor muscles.",
   "note": "Cadaveric moment-arm study that treats psoas major and iliacus separately; both act as hip flexors. Neither this paper nor the project's muscle data gives a hip ADDUCTION action for iliacus (searched 2026-10-25: PubMed, web) -- iliacus adduction is therefore not animated."
  }
 },
 "none_reasons": {
  "scapula": "scapula and clavicle are driven by the shoulder in the pose rig (fixed scapulohumeral rhythm), not posable on their own",
  "segmental": "segmental action: the pose rig moves whole spinal regions, not single vertebral segments",
  "unrigged": "no joint for this motion in the pose rig (face, eye, tongue, jaw, larynx, pharynx, ribs, pelvic floor and perineum are not rigged)",
  "nodof": "the motion it is cited for (finger or toe abduction/adduction, opposition, knee rotation) is not a degree of freedom of the pose rig",
  "noaction": "its cited actions are not joint motions"
 },
